---
import Tool from '../../layouts/Tool.astro'
import Calculator from '../../components/Calculator.astro'
import type { FaqEntry } from '../../types.ts'

const faq: FaqEntry[] = [
  {
    q: 'Can I use this calculator to avoid getting pregnant?',
    a: 'No. This calculator, and the app behind it, is not a method of contraception. It estimates a fertile window from an assumed 14-day luteal phase, and real ovulation timing varies enough — from cycle to cycle and from the average — that relying on the estimate to avoid intercourse on "unsafe" days fails often enough to be dangerous. If you need reliable contraception, use a method proven for that purpose and discuss options with a doctor or pharmacist.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the estimate count backward from my next period instead of forward from my last one?',
    a: "Because the two halves of a cycle behave differently. The stretch from the start of a period to ovulation shifts around a lot from month to month, but the stretch from ovulation to the next period is much steadier, usually close to 14 days. Counting backward from a predicted period date anchors the estimate to the more stable number; counting forward from the last period would anchor it to the least stable one.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How wide is the fertile window and why does it start before the ovulation date?',
    a: "The window runs from five days before the estimated ovulation date through one day after it — six days in total. It starts early because sperm can survive in the reproductive tract for up to about five days waiting for an egg to be released, and it extends one extra day because the egg itself is viable for roughly 24 hours after release. Conception is possible from intercourse anywhere in that span, not only on the ovulation day itself.",
  },
  {
    q: 'My ovulation test (LH strip) shows a different day than the calculator. Which one should I trust?',
    a: "Trust the test over the calculator for that specific cycle. An LH surge test detects a real hormonal event happening in your body right now; the calculator is a projection built from an assumed 14-day countdown applied to an average cycle length you typed in. When they disagree, the test is telling you something the calculator has no way to know — that this particular cycle isn't tracking the average.",
  },
  {
    q: "I have a fixed 14-day luteal phase in real life — is the estimate accurate for me?",
    a: "It should land close, but 'close' still isn't certain. A 14-day luteal phase is a population average, and even people whose luteal phase is normally very consistent see it shift by a day or two sometimes, for reasons ranging from stress to illness to nothing identifiable at all. The estimate is a best single guess built on that average, not a measurement of what actually happened inside your body this cycle.",
  },
  {
    q: 'What if my cycles are irregular — does the estimate still mean anything?',
    a: "It will still produce a date, but treat it as considerably rougher. The whole calculation rests on one cycle length repeating and one fixed countback from the next period; the less predictable your cycles actually are, the less that projection has to say about any one specific month. Signs like basal body temperature or cervical mucus changes, tracked over the cycle itself, tend to be more informative than a calendar projection when cycles don't follow a steady pattern.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the app track ovulation better than this standalone calculator?',
    a: 'The underlying arithmetic is the same, but the app applies it to your own logged cycle history rather than one manually typed cycle length, and it can incorporate corroborating signals — basal body temperature and ovulation test results — to raise or lower how confident it is in a given estimate. This page runs the calculation once, on demand, from whatever numbers you give it.',
  },
]
---
<Tool
  title="Ovulation Calculator — Estimate Your Fertile Window"
  description="Estimate ovulation and a six-day fertile window from your last period and cycle length. Runs in your browser; not a method of contraception."
  path="/tools/ovulation-calculator"
  faq={faq}
  focus={{ from: 10, to: 16, color: 'fertile', label: 'Fertile window' }}
>
  <Calculator slot="widget" kind="ovulation" />

  <p>
    An ovulation calculator answers a narrower question than most people
    expect: not "when will I ovulate," which no calendar tool can know for
    certain, but "given an average cycle length, when does ovulation most
    plausibly fall, and which days around it carry the most chance of
    conception?" Type in the first day of your last period and your average
    cycle length, and this tool projects a single estimated ovulation date
    plus a six-day fertile window around it — using the same backward-counting
    arithmetic described below. Everything runs as JavaScript in your own
    browser; the dates you enter are never sent to LunaTrack or anyone else.
    If you're also trying to work out when your next period is likely to
    start, the <a href="/tools/period-calculator" class="underline">period
    calculator</a> handles that half on its own, and the
    <a href="/tools/cycle-length-calculator" class="underline">cycle-length
    calculator</a> can help you get a more personal average to type in here in
    the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>How the date is estimated</h2>
  <p>
    The calculation takes two numbers — your last period's start date and
    your average cycle length — and produces an estimated ovulation date by
    working backward from a predicted future event rather than forward from a
    past one. First, it adds your cycle length to your last period's start
    date to get a predicted next period date, exactly the way the period
    calculator does. Then it subtracts a fixed 14 days from that predicted
    date to land on an estimated ovulation day. That 14-day figure is the
    assumed length of the luteal phase — the stretch of a cycle from
    ovulation to the start of the next period — and it is the single most
    important number in this whole calculation, because it's an assumption,
    not a measurement of anything specific to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    From that one ovulation estimate, the fertile window is built by
    subtracting five days and adding one: five days before ovulation through
    one day after it, six days altogether. There's no adjustment for how
    regular or irregular your cycles have been, no weighting of recent
    cycles, and no attempt to narrow the window based on anything other than
    the two numbers you typed in. That's deliberate — a more sophisticated
    estimate needs a real history of temperature readings, hormone tests, or
    logged cycles to draw on, not two numbers entered once, and the
    "Signs that corroborate the estimate" section below covers what that
    additional evidence looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>Why we count back from the next period, not forward from the last</h2>
  <p>
    It would be simpler, in one sense, to just count forward roughly two
    weeks from the start of your last period and call that ovulation. Plenty
    of informal advice does exactly that. It's also less accurate, and the
    reason comes down to which half of the cycle is more variable.
  </p>
  <p>
    A menstrual cycle has two phases separated by ovulation. The first phase
    — from the start of a period to ovulation — is called the follicular
    phase, and it is the part that stretches and compresses from month to
    month. Its length responds to all sorts of things: illness, stress,
    travel, sleep disruption, and plenty of ordinary biological noise that
    has nothing to do with anything being wrong. The second phase — from
    ovulation to the next period — is the luteal phase, and it is
    considerably steadier. In most people it holds close to 14 days,
    cycle after cycle, even when the first phase is bouncing around.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because the luteal phase is the more consistent half, anchoring the
    ovulation estimate to it — by counting backward a fixed 14 days from a
    predicted period date — produces a more reliable estimate than anchoring
    to the less consistent follicular phase by counting forward from the last
    period. It's the same reason the due-date calculator on this site uses a
    comparable logic in reverse; both tools lean on the part of the cycle
    that moves around the least. It does not mean the estimate is precise —
    the 14-day figure is still an average, and real luteal phases do vary by
    a day or two even in people with generally consistent cycles — only that
    counting backward is the more defensible of two imperfect options.
  </p>

  <h2>The six-day fertile window and where it comes from</h2>
  <p>
    The fertile window here spans six days: five days before the estimated
    ovulation date through one day after it. Those two numbers come from two
    separate pieces of reproductive biology, not from an arbitrary buffer
    added for caution.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sperm can survive inside the reproductive tract for up to roughly five
    days under favorable conditions, waiting for an egg to be released. That
    means intercourse several days before ovulation can still result in
    conception if sperm are still viable when the egg appears — hence the
    window opening five days early. On the other side, an egg released at
    ovulation remains viable for roughly 24 hours before it's no longer able
    to be fertilized, which is where the single extra day after the
    ovulation estimate comes from. Put together, six days is the span during
    which intercourse has a realistic chance of leading to conception, given
    a single estimated ovulation date sitting at its center.
  </p>
  <p>
    It's worth being precise about what this window is not. It isn't a claim
    that conception is equally likely on all six days — the days immediately
    before ovulation are generally considered to carry the highest chance,
    tapering off toward the edges of the window — and it isn't a claim that
    conception is impossible outside it, only markedly less likely. It also
    isn't six actual observed days from your own cycle; it's six days
    computed around one projected date built from an assumed luteal phase
    length. Both layers of estimation stack, which is part of why this
    window should be read as a planning guide rather than a precise
    biological boundary.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs that corroborate the estimate (BBT, cervical mucus, LH tests)</h2>
  <p>
    Because the calendar estimate here rests entirely on an assumed 14-day
    luteal phase applied to a typed-in average, it's worth knowing what
    actual physical signs can tell you that a calculator can't. None of these
    require throwing out the calendar estimate — they're most useful as a way
    to check it against what your body is actually doing in a given cycle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Basal body temperature (BBT) is your resting temperature taken first
    thing each morning, before getting out of bed. It typically rises by a
    few tenths of a degree after ovulation and stays elevated through the
    rest of the luteal phase, because of the temperature-raising effect of
    the hormone progesterone. A sustained rise across several consecutive
    mornings is a signal that ovulation has already happened in that cycle —
    it confirms the event after the fact rather than predicting it in
    advance, which is a meaningfully different kind of information than a
    calendar projection.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cervical mucus changes across a cycle in a pattern that's fairly
    consistent from person to person: it tends to become clearer, wetter, and
    more stretchable — often compared to raw egg white — in the days
    approaching ovulation, then dries up again afterward. Noticing that
    change happening earlier or later than the calculator's estimate is a
    useful, low-cost way to sense-check the projection in real time, rather
    than only after the fact the way a temperature rise does.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ovulation predictor kits (LH tests) detect the surge in luteinizing
    hormone that triggers ovulation, usually 24 to 36 hours before it
    happens. Because they detect an actual hormonal event rather than
    projecting from an average, a positive test is generally more reliable
    for a specific cycle than the calendar estimate on this page — and if the
    two disagree, the test result deserves more weight for that cycle,
    because it's measuring something the calculator has no access to.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of these signs turn the estimate into certainty, and none of them
    are things this calculator can see or account for — it only knows the
    two numbers you typed in. But combined, especially over more than one
    cycle, they build a picture of your own pattern that a single calendar
    projection, done once, cannot.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this is not contraception</h2>
  <p>
    This is the most important thing on this page, so it's worth stating
    plainly and without hedging: this calculator, and the LunaTrack app it
    comes from, is not a method of contraception. Do not use the estimated
    fertile window, on its own, to decide which days are "safe" to avoid
    pregnancy.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reasoning is the same reasoning that runs through every section
    above. The estimate depends on an assumed 14-day luteal phase and a
    cycle length you typed in by hand, both of which are averages rather than
    measurements of what's actually happening in a given cycle. Ovulation
    timing shifts in response to stress, illness, travel, and plenty of
    causes that leave no advance warning, and it can occur several days
    earlier or later than a calendar projection expects — including,
    occasionally, outside the six-day window this calculator draws. Fertility
    awareness methods that are used for contraception in a clinically
    supervised way combine daily temperature and mucus observations, taken
    in the moment, with strict protocols for how much margin to build in —
    they are not the same thing as a single calendar calculation done once
    in advance, and even those supervised methods carry a real, documented
    failure rate when used to prevent pregnancy.
  </p>
  <p>
    If avoiding pregnancy matters to you, use a method with a known and
    acceptable failure rate for that purpose, and talk to a doctor or
    pharmacist about what fits your situation. If achieving pregnancy is the
    goal instead, this estimate is a reasonable starting point for timing,
    and the corroborating signs above are worth adding to it rather than
    relying on the calendar date alone.
  </p>

  <h2>When to seek advice</h2>
  <p>
    A few situations are worth raising with a doctor rather than working
    through with a calculator. If you've been trying to conceive for twelve
    months without success — or six months if you're over 35 — that's
    generally the point at which a fertility evaluation is recommended,
    because a calendar estimate can't distinguish ordinary month-to-month
    variation from an underlying reason conception isn't happening.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your cycles are irregular enough that you can't confidently name an
    average cycle length to type into this calculator, that irregularity
    itself is worth mentioning at an appointment — it can have identifiable
    causes, and a calendar tool has nothing useful to say about why a cycle
    doesn't follow a predictable pattern. Similarly, if BBT charting or LH
    tests consistently disagree with where a calendar estimate places
    ovulation, or seem to show no clear ovulation event at all across several
    cycles, that pattern is more informative discussed with a clinician than
    puzzled over alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    And regardless of what any calculator or test suggests, pelvic pain,
    unusually heavy or prolonged bleeding, or bleeding between periods are
    reasons to see a doctor on their own terms — they're not something this
    page, or any fertility calculator, is built to evaluate. For a broader
    look at what LunaTrack can help track between appointments, including
    corroborating signals like BBT and test results, see the
    <a href="/features" class="underline">features page</a>; for how any of
    that data is stored and who can see it, see
    <a href="/privacy" class="underline">how your data is handled</a>.
  </p>
</Tool>
